## 14. RBAC, ABAC, and Session Tags

### RBAC vs ABAC

RBAC:


- Access comes from an assigned role or group.
- It is easy to understand.
- It can create too many roles when many permission combinations are needed.

Example:
Finance-DEV-Read
Finance-DEV-Write
Finance-PROD-Read
Finance-PROD-Write
HR-DEV-Read
...

ABAC:

- Access depends on attributes, usually tags, on the caller and the resource.
- Core comparison:

    aws:PrincipalTag/Department
    == aws:ResourceTag/Department

- One policy can cover many callers and resources when tags are used
  consistently.

### Session Tags

- AWS can add session tags when a caller assumes a role.
- IAM reads them as principal tags.
- They can describe department, project, environment, tenant, or cost center.
- A transitive tag can pass to the next role in a role chain.
- A non-transitive tag disappears at the next hop.

### Evaluation and Governance

- If the required tags match, the Allow statement can apply.
- If they do not match, that Allow is ignored. The request is implicitly denied
  when no other Allow exists.
- A matching explicit Deny still wins.
- ABAC is secure only when tag values are trustworthy.
- A caller who can change resource tags or choose any session tag may give
  themselves access.
- Restrict who can tag roles and resources.
- Also restrict which session-tag keys and transitive tags callers may pass.

